  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Employment policy developed and Employment mainstreamed in the national development framework</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Namibia is an upper-middle income economy, which however suffers from several challenges including high unemployment that are significantly higher among youths at 43% and 48.5% among young women, with women dominating employment in the informal sector at 61.2%; and estimated 70% of the population not having access to electricity, widespread poverty, as well as being one of the most unequal societies in the world. The Government of Namibia has embarked on several ambitious plans to address these grave challenges, albeit with mixed success (as evidenced by the ILO-supported independent evaluation of the 2nd National Employment Policy). 

Against this backdrop, high hopes are being placed on the development of a large-scale green hydrogen industry to support national economic growth and development as well as to contribute to addressing climate change and global decarbonisation goals. Namibia has been identified as one of top three locations for green hydrogen production due to its high potential for hybrid solar photo voltaic and onshore wind production as renewable energy sources for the green hydrogen production. A multi-billion-dollar HYPHEN Southern Corridor Development Initiative (SCDI) Namibian Green Hydrogen Project is being developed in phases aiming to produce 300,000 metric tons of green hydrogen per year. There is an expectation that these investments will result in employment and socio-economic benefits for local communities, but there is a risk that these will not come about without clear support in several areas. The RBSA initiative seeks to support constituents to reap these benefits for Namibian women and men. 

The Ministry of Labour, Industrial Relations and Employment Creation and the National Planning Commission have requested assistance from the ILO to ensure that the investment leads to social impacts and results in employment and skills development opportunities for Namibians. Further support requests relate to formulating the 3rd National E</narrative>
  </description>
